intent: >
  Establish the cross-platform package contract (ownership rules + capability
  matrix) as a reviewable doc before any installer code changes.
slice: 2/6
kind: chore
behavior_change: none
reversibility: trivial
scope:
  declared:
    - docs/package-matrix.md
  actual:
    - docs/package-matrix.md
  drift: []
proof:
  tests_run: "awk pipe-count check on the markdown table"
  result: green
  failing: 0
tests:
  needed: "no"
  rationale: >
    Documentation only, no executable change. Verified the matrix table has a
    consistent column count across all rows.
specifications:
  - "Parity is by capability, not package manager: macOS=Homebrew, Ubuntu=apt/vendor-apt/official-binary, runtimes in topic installers, Homebrew-on-Linux only as explicit exception"
  - "AWS CLI on Ubuntu = official v2 installer, never apt (apt ships v1)"
  - "GitHub CLI on Ubuntu = GitHub official apt repo (avoid distro lag)"
  - "Yarn via Corepack on both; never `apt install yarn` (installs cmdtest)"
  - "Version column contract: exact (pinned both OSes) | major (compatible line) | latest"
  - "Matrix must be updated in the same PR that adds/removes/moves a shared tool"
deviations_from_plan: none
trade_offs: >
  "Split the package work into contract (this slice) then installers (PR 2B) so
  reviewers can argue the ownership model before scripts depend on it."
self_review:
  consistency: "Encodes the operating model from the approved plan verbatim; no new decisions introduced here."
confidence: high
reviewer_attention:
  - "docs/package-matrix.md — the Version column (exact vs major) per row is the judgment worth a second look; runtimes marked exact, Terraform exact, CLIs major"
